<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>Rajiv Nagar P.S. Case No. 119 of 2026, District Patna, was registered on the complaint of a supplier of laundry-business materials. The informant alleged that the petitioner and co-accused, claiming to be directors of Rondella Laundromat Private Limited, obtained chemicals and other articles worth about Rs 11 lakh without payment; a cheque of Rs 3 lakh issued towards the dues bounced; and the persons associated with the company closed their office and fled. The case was registered under Sections 316(2) and 318(4) BNS with Section 138 of the NI Act.</p>

<h2>The petitioner's answer</h2>
<p>The petitioner, in custody since 20 April 2026, drew a line between designation and control:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>A professional director.</strong> He said he was introduced as a professional director, joined as an employee before taking the directorship, and was not a promoter of the company.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Not his cheque.</strong> The dishonoured cheque was not issued by him, and — notably — the company itself was not arraigned as an accused in the case.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Others ran the money.</strong> The financial and managerial affairs, he said, were handled by the co-accused and their family; one co-accused had already secured anticipatory bail from a coordinate Bench in September 2026.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Antecedents explained.</strong> Six earlier cases existed, but all flowed from the same directorship, with no criminal record before it.</div>
</div>
<p>The State and the informant opposed, pressing the Rs 11 lakh loss and the directors' representations.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>
<p>The Court directed release on bail, weighing four factors expressly: the vague nature of the allegation against the petitioner specifically, his status in the company, the period of custody, and the submission of the chargesheet. The conditions are standard Patna practice: a bond of Rs 10,000 with two sureties before the ACJM-II, Patna; one bailor to be a close relative; attendance on every date the trial court requires; and cancellation exposure under Section 480(3) BNSS if the petitioner absents himself on three consecutive dates or breaches the terms.</p>
