<h2>The wider pattern: unpaid dues and the criminal process</h2>
<p>Cases of this shape sit at the junction of civil recovery and criminal law. A supplier left unpaid has civil remedies — a summary suit, a commercial suit, or a Section 138 NI Act complaint against the drawer of the cheque — and often adds a cheating and breach-of-trust FIR to bring pressure. At the bail stage, courts increasingly ask the question this order asked: what, precisely, did this accused do beyond holding an office? For persons accepting directorships in small companies, the case is also a quiet warning — a professional or nominee directorship carries prosecution risk when the company defaults, and documented distance from financial operations is what later persuades a bail court.</p>
<div class="note">Bail observations do not decide the trial. Whether the petitioner induced the supplies or merely wore a title is a question for the trial court on evidence — the High Court's order is confined to his liberty pending that determination.</div>
